{
  "seam": "C6",
  "name": "out-bytes-committed-after-reopen",
  "mark": {
    "required-green": "e1"
  },
  "kind": "instance",
  "grade": "reference",
  "graded_by": [
    {
      "unit": "U2",
      "test": "core/internal/record/gap_test.go — TestReopenCountsPastTheCommittedOffset: after a reopen cut, out_bytes_committed equals the kept recording's code-0 payload plus its gap markers' byte counts, and recording_last_seq_out names the last output frame kept (E1 persists both in one write at the restart; from contract-v0.3 that write is the realign, with a grid snapshot or record-only, and that half is owed by the E2 core plan)"
    }
  ],
  "description": "out_bytes_committed describes exactly the point recording_last_seq_out describes: the output payload bytes through that frame, declared losses included. After a crash the reopen rule cuts the recording to the last frame both of its files hold, and never back to the commit point; the core then counts the recording forward through what it kept — here 19456 bytes of code-0 payload and one gap marker declaring 1024 — and the count and the seq are only ever persisted together, in one write. From contract-v0.3 a restart persists them only in its realign before hello: with a grid snapshot, or record-only for a session with no snapshot (continuity-state top). A count that drifted from the seq would make the next gap marker state a false byte count, which is what immortality §4.3 exists to prevent.",
  "corpus": [
    "immortality §4.1",
    "immortality §4.3",
    "Storage §8.6"
  ],
